#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161764 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161972 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8919d471-0e4c-55c1-b39e-2b46a86540d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Solution of a Linear Diophantine Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "41efb6ec-beb7-52ff-9951-fd73454e50d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-8134" :question/problem "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $3x+8y=5$?\n\n1. $(7,-2)$\n2. $(22,-8)$\n3. $(15,-5)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(7,-2)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=7$ and $y=-2$, we obtain\n$3x + 8y|= 5; 3 \\cdot 7 + 8 \\cdot (-2)|= 5; 21 - 16|= 5; 5|= 5✓$\n- $(22,-8)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=22$ and $y=-8$, we obtain\n$3x + 8y|= 5; 3 \\cdot 22 + 8 \\cdot (-8)|= 5; 66 - 64|= 5; 2 \\mid \\ne 5\\cdot$\n- $(15,-5)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=15$ and $y=-5$, we obtain\n$3x + 8y|= 5; 3 \\cdot 15 + 8 \\cdot (-5)|= 5; 45 - 40|= 5; 5|= 5✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161765 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161973 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "607df418-165b-5d37-aae1-d49ce8e9d3e5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Linear Diophantine Equation Has a Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "b156d26b-369f-57d7-8fcf-3e67f2c03e60" :question/math-academy-id "e-8135" :question/problem "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n\n1. $18x+15y=24$\n2. $7x-28y=10$\n3. $9x+27y=16$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d \\: \\mid \\: c$, where\n\n$$\nd = \\text{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has solutions. We have $a=18$, $b=15$, and $c=24$. Since\n$d=\\text{gcd}(18,15)=3$\nand $3 \\: \\mid \\: 24$, our equation has solutions.\n- Equation II has no solution. We have $a=7$, $b=-28$, and $c=10$. Since\n$d=\\text{gcd}(7,-28)=7$\nand $7 \\!\\not{\\mid}\\: 10$, our equation has no solution.\n- Equation III has no solution. We have $a=9$, $b=27$, and $c=16$. Since\n$d=\\text{gcd}(9,27)=9$\nand $9 \\!\\not{\\mid}\\: 16$, our equation has no solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161766 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161974 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b68b2579-6ae9-53a4-a9f1-b36ff041d500" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Linear Diophantine Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "5771c3fd-b47b-5f51-bc0c-d87771df70d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-8136" :question/problem "Consider the Diophantine equation $2x+7y=10$. The integer pair $(\\overset{\\sim}{x},\\overset{\\sim}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x$. What is the value of $\\overset{\\sim}{x}$?" :question/worked-solution "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=2$, $b=7$, and $c=10$. We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(2,7)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7 &= \\begin{vmatrix}2 \\cdot 3 & +\\end{vmatrix}1 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 2 &= \\end{vmatrix}1 \\cdot 2 \\mid + \\mid 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 7 - 2 \\cdot 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(a,b) = 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot (-3) + 7 = 1\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 \\cdot (-3) + 7 &= 1 \\\\\n2 \\cdot (-3) \\cdot 10 + 7 \\cdot 10 &= 10 \\\\\n2 \\cdot (-30) + 7 \\cdot 10 &= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(-30,10)\n$$\n\nis a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left(-30+\\dfrac{b}{d}t, \\, 10-\\dfrac{a}{d}t\\right) = (-30+7t, \\, 10-2t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nt=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\,\n$$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of\n\n$$\nx=-30+7t\n$$\n\nis attained when $t=5$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\sim}{x} = -30+7 \\cdot 5 = 5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161767 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161975 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "096b8bc9-e3fc-55fa-8266-5cafa411a2f6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Linear Diophantine Equation Given One of Its Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "1b1f251a-cb4e-5af9-b7ab-fddfbfc45faa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8138" :question/problem "Given that $(x,y)=(15,14)$ is a solution of the Diophantine equation $11x-13y=-17$, and that $(\\overset{\\sim}{x},\\overset{\\sim}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x$, what is the value of $\\overset{\\sim}{x}$?" :question/worked-solution "First, recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d \\: \\mid \\: c$, where\n\n$$\nd = \\text{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $(x_0,y_0)$ is a solution to the equation, then the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(x_0+\\dfrac{b}{d}t, \\, y_0-\\dfrac{a}{d}t\\right), \\qquad t=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=11$, $b=-13$, $c=-17$, and\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(11,-13)=1\n$$\n\nSince $(15,14)$ is a solution of\n\n$$\n11x-13y=-17\n$$\n\nthe general solution must be\n\n$$\n\\left(15+\\dfrac{(-13)}{1}t, \\, 14-\\dfrac{11}{1}t\\right) = (15-13t, \\, 14-11t)\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nt=0, \\pm1, \\pm2, \\ldots \\,\n$$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=15-13t$ is attained when $t=1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\sim}{x} = 15-13 \\cdot 1 = 2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162610 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161974 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b68b2579-6ae9-53a4-a9f1-b36ff041d500" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Linear Diophantine Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99045d38-a490-54c0-b373-341855fc30c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160953} {:answer/id #uuid "d600bb19-1ac6-5aee-a07a-3a01a7701761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160954} {:answer/id #uuid "0a212525-4cf7-5285-bd4c-14473712ced4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160955} {:answer/id #uuid "51302934-3c74-5cb2-9eb7-891902b6daf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160956} {:answer/id #uuid "61bbb7c1-cd0b-5060-ab1c-bba28d16b30a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160957}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fd7111de-7240-5214-91ff-4e944eb7cfac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160952}] :question/id #uuid "111dc48c-89a1-58b9-b26b-03172f489d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93880" :question/problem "Consider the Diophantine equation $6x + 8y = 10$. The integer pair $(x^(∼), y^(∼))$ is the solution with the greatest possible negative value of $x$. What is the value of $x^(∼)$?"}]}
